 "lines": [
  {
   "scene": 1,
   "text": "What if Claude could understand any program… even without the source code?",
   "start": 0.15,
   "end": 4.033
  },
  {
   "scene": 2,
   "text": "A binary is just this: millions of bytes.",
   "start": 4.483,
   "end": 6.957
  },
  {
   "scene": 2,
   "text": "You can't read it like this, and neither can an AI.",
   "start": 7.137,
   "end": 9.804
  },
  {
   "scene": 3,
   "text": "That's what REA, Reverse Engineer Anything, is for.",
   "start": 10.254,
   "end": 13.347
  },
  {
   "scene": 3,
   "text": "It's an MCP server that gives Claude over 200 reverse-engineering tools and connects it to real engines like Ghidra, IDA, Hopper and JADX.",
   "start": 13.527,
   "end": 23.255
  },
  {
   "scene": 4,
   "text": "Step one: open_binary.",
   "start": 23.705,
   "end": 25.199
  },
  {
   "scene": 4,
   "text": "REA works out what kind of file it is and picks the engine:",
   "start": 25.379,
   "end": 28.728
  },
  {
   "scene": 4,
   "text": "Ghidra, IDA or Hopper for native code, JADX for Android, Binwalk for firmware.",
   "start": 28.908,
   "end": 34.604
  },
  {
   "scene": 4,
   "text": "For an Electron app, it analyzes the JavaScript directly.",
   "start": 34.784,
   "end": 38.496
  },
  {
   "scene": 5,
   "text": "Step two: map it.",
   "start": 38.946,
   "end": 40.034
  },
  {
   "scene": 5,
   "text": "Claude sees the architecture, the libraries it uses and the text inside it.",
   "start": 40.214,
   "end": 44.651
  },
  {
   "scene": 5,
   "text": "It finds a message like ‘License invalid’ and follows its cross-references to the function that uses it.",
   "start": 44.831,
   "end": 50.911
  },
  {
   "scene": 6,
   "text": "Step three: decompile.",
   "start": 51.361,
   "end": 52.748
  },
  {
   "scene": 6,
   "text": "The assembly becomes C pseudocode, and the call graph shows which function calls which.",
   "start": 52.928,
   "end": 57.941
  },
  {
   "scene": 7,
   "text": "Step four: understand it.",
   "start": 58.391,
   "end": 59.863
  },
  {
   "scene": 7,
   "text": "Claude gives functions real names and adds comments.",
   "start": 60.043,
   "end": 62.902
  },
  {
   "scene": 7,
   "text": "Every conclusion is backed by an Evidence ID, separating what it saw from what it inferred and what it still doesn't know.",
   "start": 63.082,
   "end": 70.165
  },
  {
   "scene": 8,
   "text": "When static analysis isn't enough, REA watches the program while it runs, using Frida, GDB or the browser.",
   "start": 70.615,
   "end": 77.975
  },
  {
   "scene": 9,
   "text": "Step five: rebuild.",
   "start": 78.425,
   "end": 79.705
  },
  {
   "scene": 9,
   "text": "Using all that evidence, Claude writes clean source code in a normal project.",
   "start": 79.885,
   "end": 84.407
  },
  {
   "scene": 9,
   "text": "Then REA checks that the new code meets every documented behavior.",
   "start": 84.587,
   "end": 88.705
  },
  {
   "scene": 10,
   "text": "And now it's just regular code.",
   "start": 89.155,
   "end": 90.712
  },
  {
   "scene": 10,
   "text": "Claude can change it, build it and test it.",
   "start": 90.892,
   "end": 92.919
  },
  {
   "scene": 11,
   "text": "Binary → evidence → code.",
   "start": 93.369,
   "end": 94.755
  },
  {
   "scene": 11,
   "text": "Follow for more tools like this.",
   "start": 94.935,
   "end": 96.621
  }
 ],
